From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:27:07 +0900
Subject: Do not check captures in a run which reads its units from TASTy

A unit compiled with separation checking did not recompile from its TASTy
with the same options. The checker gives an inferred type the capture sets it
finds for it, and takes a type that was written to say all there is to say.
TASTy does not record which types were inferred, so in an unpickled tree
every type reads as written: `val boxed = new Array[AnyRef](n)` declares a
read-only array, which cannot be updated, and the synthesized members of an
enum are held to types they were never meant to be checked against. The
checker rejected what it had accepted as source.

Leaving the options out is no remedy, since other phases go by them, and
`-Yskip:cc` crashed: the setup phase stayed, as a phase and as a symbol
transformer, and asked the missing checker for its state.

The setup phase, its symbol transformer and the checker are now inactive in a
run which reads its units from TASTy, where the units were checked when they
were compiled, and in a run without the checker's phase. The options are
otherwise as they were, so the phases which go by them emit the same code.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: tastynocc
